**1. Right-Sizing Resources**

Analyze actual usage and adjust instance sizes.

```java
// Monitor resource usage
@Service
public class ResourceMonitoringService {
  @Autowired
  private CloudWatchClient cloudWatch;

  public ResourceUtilization getUtilization(String instanceId, int days) {
    Instant end = Instant.now();
    Instant start = end.minus(days, ChronoUnit.DAYS);

    // Get CPU utilization
    GetMetricStatisticsRequest cpuRequest = GetMetricStatisticsRequest.builder()
      .namespace("AWS/EC2")
      .metricName("CPUUtilization")
      .dimensions(Dimension.builder()
        .name("InstanceId")
        .value(instanceId)
        .build())
      .startTime(start)
      .endTime(end)
      .period(3600) // 1 hour
      .statistics(Statistic.AVERAGE, Statistic.MAXIMUM)
      .build();

    GetMetricStatisticsResponse cpuResponse = cloudWatch.getMetricStatistics(cpuRequest);

    // Analyze: If avg CPU < 30%, downsize instance
    double avgCpu = cpuResponse.datapoints().stream()
      .mapToDouble(Datapoint::average)
      .average()
      .orElse(0);

    if (avgCpu < 30) {
      log.warn("Instance {} underutilized: {}% CPU", instanceId, avgCpu);
      // Recommendation: Downsize from t3.large to t3.medium
    }

    return new ResourceUtilization(avgCpu, maxCpu, avgMemory);
  }
}
```

Rule: Average CPU < 40% for 7 days → Downsize. Average CPU > 80% → Upsize.

**2. Use Reserved Instances / Savings Plans**

```markdown
## On-Demand vs Reserved Pricing (t3.large)
- On-Demand: $0.0832/hour = $730/month
- 1-Year Reserved: $0.049/hour = $430/month (41% savings)
- 3-Year Reserved: $0.031/hour = $272/month (63% savings)

## Savings Plan (more flexible)
- Commit to $X/hour for 1-3 years
- Applies across instance types, regions
- Example: $500/month commitment = 30% savings

## Strategy
- Baseline workload: Reserved Instances (predictable)
- Variable workload: On-Demand or Spot
- Development: On-Demand (used during business hours)
```

**3. Use Spot Instances**

```java
// AWS Batch with Spot instances
@Configuration
public class BatchConfig {
  @Bean
  public ComputeEnvironment spotComputeEnvironment() {
    return ComputeEnvironment.builder()
      .computeEnvironmentName("spot-environment")
      .type(CEType.MANAGED)
      .state(CEState.ENABLED)
      .computeResources(ComputeResource.builder()
        .type(CRType.SPOT)
        .minvCpus(0)
        .maxvCpus(256)
        .desiredvCpus(0)
        .instanceTypes("optimal") // Let AWS choose
        .spotIamFleetRole("arn:aws:iam::123456789:role/aws-batch-spot-fleet-role")
        .bidPercentage(70) // Bid 70% of on-demand price
        .build())
      .build();
  }
}

// ECS with Spot capacity
resource "aws_ecs_capacity_provider" "spot" {
  name = "spot-capacity-provider"

  auto_scaling_group_provider {
    auto_scaling_group_arn = aws_autoscaling_group.spot.arn

    managed_scaling {
      maximum_scaling_step_size = 10
      minimum_scaling_step_size = 1
      status = "ENABLED"
      target_capacity = 80
    }
  }
}

# Use for non-critical workloads
# Savings: 70-90% off on-demand price
# Risk: Can be terminated with 2-minute warning
```

Use Spot for: Batch processing, CI/CD builds, data processing, stateless workers.
Don't use Spot for: Databases, critical real-time services.

**4. Auto-Scaling**

```yaml
# ECS Auto-Scaling
resource "aws_appautoscaling_target" "ecs_target" {
  max_capacity = 10
  min_capacity = 2  # Keep 2 during off-hours
  resource_id = "service/${aws_ecs_cluster.main.name}/${aws_ecs_service.app.name}"
  scalable_dimension = "ecs:service:DesiredCount"
  service_namespace = "ecs"
}

# Scale based on CPU
resource "aws_appautoscaling_policy" "ecs_cpu" {
  name = "cpu-scaling"
  policy_type = "TargetTrackingScaling"
  resource_id = aws_appautoscaling_target.ecs_target.resource_id
  scalable_dimension = aws_appautoscaling_target.ecs_target.scalable_dimension
  service_namespace = aws_appautoscaling_target.ecs_target.service_namespace

  target_tracking_scaling_policy_configuration {
    target_value = 70.0  # Target 70% CPU

    predefined_metric_specification {
      predefined_metric_type = "ECSServiceAverageCPUUtilization"
    }

    scale_in_cooldown = 300   # 5 min before scaling down
    scale_out_cooldown = 60   # 1 min before scaling up
  }
}

# Schedule-based scaling (office hours)
resource "aws_appautoscaling_scheduled_action" "scale_up" {
  name = "scale-up-morning"
  service_namespace = aws_appautoscaling_target.ecs_target.service_namespace
  resource_id = aws_appautoscaling_target.ecs_target.resource_id
  scalable_dimension = aws_appautoscaling_target.ecs_target.scalable_dimension
  schedule = "cron(0 7 * * MON-FRI *)"  # 7 AM weekdays

  scalable_target_action {
    min_capacity = 5
    max_capacity = 10
  }
}

resource "aws_appautoscaling_scheduled_action" "scale_down" {
  name = "scale-down-evening"
  schedule = "cron(0 19 * * MON-FRI *)"  # 7 PM weekdays

  scalable_target_action {
    min_capacity = 2
    max_capacity = 5
  }
}
```

**5. Optimize Storage**

```markdown
## S3 Storage Classes
- Standard: $0.023/GB (frequent access)
- Intelligent-Tiering: $0.023/GB + $0.0025/1000 objects (auto-tier)
- Infrequent Access: $0.0125/GB (access < 1/month)
- Glacier: $0.004/GB (archive, minutes to hours retrieval)
- Glacier Deep Archive: $0.00099/GB (long-term archive, 12h retrieval)

## Lifecycle Policy
{
  "Rules": [{
    "Id": "archive-old-logs",
    "Status": "Enabled",
    "Transitions": [
      {
        "Days": 30,
        "StorageClass": "STANDARD_IA"
      },
      {
        "Days": 90,
        "StorageClass": "GLACIER"
      }
    ],
    "Expiration": {
      "Days": 365
    }
  }]
}

## EBS Volume Optimization
- gp3: $0.08/GB (default, good for most)
- gp2: $0.10/GB (legacy)
- io2: $0.125/GB + $0.065/IOPS (high performance)
- st1: $0.045/GB (throughput-optimized, big data)
- sc1: $0.015/GB (cold storage, infrequent access)

## RDS Storage
- Delete old snapshots (keep last 7 days)
- Use Aurora Serverless for variable workloads
- Enable storage auto-scaling
```

**6. Use Serverless**

```markdown
## Lambda Pricing
- First 1M requests/month: FREE
- After: $0.20 per 1M requests
- Duration: $0.0000166667/GB-second

## Example: Low-traffic API
- Traditional: t3.small EC2 = $15/month (always running)
- Lambda: 100K requests × 500ms × 512MB = $1/month

## Savings: 93%

## Use Lambda for:
- Infrequent workloads (<100 req/min)
- Event-driven processing
- Scheduled jobs
```

**7. Database Optimization**

```markdown
## RDS
- Use Reserved Instances (40-60% savings)
- Aurora Serverless for variable workloads
- Read replicas for read-heavy workloads
- Stop dev/test databases overnight

## Example: db.t3.medium
- On-Demand: $0.068/hour = $50/month
- 1-Year Reserved: $0.041/hour = $30/month (40% savings)

## DynamoDB
- On-Demand: Pay per request ($1.25 per million reads)
- Provisioned: Pay for capacity ($0.00065/hour per RCU)
- Use On-Demand for unpredictable traffic
- Use Provisioned for steady traffic (cheaper at scale)
```

**8. Monitoring & Alerts**

```java
@Service
public class CostMonitoringService {
  @Autowired
  private CostExplorerClient costExplorer;

  @Scheduled(cron = "0 0 9 * * MON") // Monday 9 AM
  public void weeklyC ostReport() {
    LocalDate end = LocalDate.now();
    LocalDate start = end.minusDays(7);

    GetCostAndUsageRequest request = GetCostAndUsageRequest.builder()
      .timePeriod(DateInterval.builder()
        .start(start.toString())
        .end(end.toString())
        .build())
      .granularity(Granularity.DAILY)
      .metrics("UnblendedCost")
      .groupBy(GroupDefinition.builder()
        .type(GroupDefinitionType.DIMENSION)
        .key("SERVICE")
        .build())
      .build();

    GetCostAndUsageResponse response = costExplorer.getCostAndUsage(request);

    // Analyze cost by service
    Map<String, Double> costByService = analyzeCost(response);

    // Alert if anomaly
    double totalCost = costByService.values().stream()
      .mapToDouble(Double::doubleValue)
      .sum();

    if (totalCost > WEEKLY_BUDGET) {
      alerting.sendAlert(
        "Cost Alert",
        "Weekly cost $" + totalCost + " exceeds budget $" + WEEKLY_BUDGET
      );
    }
  }
}

// AWS Budgets
resource "aws_budgets_budget" "monthly" {
